  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM EDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\n- Mostly dry through much of Friday\n\nWe remain in the same pattern for another 24-36 hours as we have \nseen much of this week thus far. That pattern is one where the \nnorthern branch of the upper jet is centered north of the area with \na flow from the WNW. The upper jet is just close enough that we are \nseeing additional cloud cover with weak short waves passing by to \nour north. A lack of cloud cover and deep instability is keeping us \nmainly dry, except for a few sprinkles. \n\nThe latest set of model guidance is keeping most of any sprinkles \njust north of the forecast area border through much of the day on \nFriday. We see a change with the upper air pattern at that time with \nupper flow taking on a more WSW flow with the ridge lifting up \ntoward us. As this occurs, a sfc warm front will be moving through \nFriday evening, and will increase the chance of rain some for much \nof the area. This is still a low chance, as moisture below 8k ft is \nstill quite limited.\n\nThis will set the stage for a warm October day with widespread 70s, \nand maybe an 80 degree reading or two across the south away from \nLake Michigan.\n\n- Rain possible on Sunday with the tropical system\n\nWe have been watching the evolution of forecasts with the Tropical \nsystem Isaias over the past few days going from missing the area \ncompletely to the SE, to now possibly bringing some rain in across \nsouthern Lower Michigan on Sunday.\n\nThis has been complicated as there is a non-tropical upper low over \nSE Texas, and a long wave trough coming in from the West Coast. \nInitially, Isaias was being pushed to the NE quickly with the long \nwave trough. Now the upper low over TX absorbs Isaias and rotates \nalmost due north before the upper wave comes in and sweeps it out. \nAs has been mentioned the past few days, rotating upper low tracks \nare difficult to forecast. The trend has definitely been for a \nbetter chance for rain, as the system goes extra-tropical and looks \nmore like a mid-latitdue system. \n\n- Turning much colder next week with a strong front late Monday\n\nHowever the system moves in Sunday, and any rain that falls will be \non its way out by late Sunday. We will see a narrow upper ridge \nbuild in briefly between the extra-tropical system, and the larger \ntrough coming in from the NW. This trough will have reinforcement \ncoming in from the NW. The front will move in Monday evening, and \npass through Tuesday. The strong forcing ahead of this trough will \nbring a bout of rain to the area.\n\nBehind the system on Tuesday and beyond, we will see a fairly decent \nCanadian high set up and bring some much cooler air in over the \narea. Highs are likely to be only in the 50s, and some lake effect \nrain will be possible.",
